#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148590} :activity/id #uuid "57371a77-6667-5bdd-b857-9b52cc9a92ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048313 :topic/id #uuid "93cbb547-bfa6-5649-8003-160f883bcb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 2467} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147392 :question/id #uuid "4447e034-350f-5e6b-a501-0f57a3888bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414710}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders"} :step/id #uuid "2317aeba-3862-5ebe-a106-96c1e216914d" :step/math-academy-id 33549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147290 :question/id #uuid "319d252c-a8c4-5a5f-ad2b-f4bdb5702b75" :question/math-academy-id "e-2662"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8fd3c2c-3340-50df-a95c-3b82f165a071" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050585}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147899}] :knowledge-point/title "Naming Prisms"} :step/id #uuid "19df8c7e-89cb-5578-919d-65b12d903d26" :step/math-academy-id 8908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147291 :question/id #uuid "0583c82a-049c-51e6-aebc-bcd32423824e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2668"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9d83422-a877-5de8-bc55-2c7aae9eeb72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414712}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Polyhedra"} :step/id #uuid "a01f473c-01ac-5a25-a94d-294c2cc662a2" :step/math-academy-id 8914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148714 :tutorial/content "A **polyhedron** is a $3$-dimensional shape where each side is a polygon. These flat polygonal sides are called **faces**.\n\n**Prisms** are polyhedrons made of two identical polygonal faces (called **bases**) connected by equal parallel line segments. If these line segments are perpendicular to the bases, we have a **right** prism.\n\nLet's take a look at some common prisms.\n\n- A **triangular (right) prism** consists of two identical triangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\n![](images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png)\n\n- A **rectangular (right) prism** consists of two identical rectangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments. Notice that each face is a rectangle.\n\n![](images/39/39ea3dd56722c7fb05ba275a054ff46479e3ee96851dafdd66fe09455a517f1a.png)\n\n- A **cube** is a special case of a rectangular prism that consists of six equal square faces.\n\n![](images/41/415d03a9a886793eee4b4d16e50af26002c0881279481b2e70773b8b7bff02a5.png)\n\nBy adding more sides to the polygonal base, we can make more prisms. For example, the following shape is a pentagonal prism.\n\n![](images/8f/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\nWe can also construct hexagonal prisms, heptagonal prisms, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "af52fff0-b45a-59d7-85f8-cacc3a9cb9f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2202 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a54ee23c-aa2f-55da-8cdb-fd96e504ff67" :step/math-academy-id 8915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148718 :tutorial/content "**Pyramids** are polyhedrons made of a polygonal base, with the other triangular faces meeting at a single point.\n\nLet's take a look at some common pyramids.\n\n- A **triangular pyramid** has a triangular base with $3$ other triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/d8/d8597d1e526e2af72255f7dce1cc13e01cf76f1c53522003b126be67a0a6c55d.png)\n\n- A **rectangular pyramid** has a rectangular base with $4$ triangular faces that meet at a single point. If the base is a square, we call this a **square pyramid.**\n\n![](images/26/26700acaf9eae64227988ddcfb43269c0eba6f6447229ec8854cc1ebfeab3334.png)\n\n- A **pentagonal pyramid** has a pentagonal base with $5$ triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nSimilarly to prisms, we can add more sides to the polygonal base to make more types of pyramids." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ebd5b00-1afa-5ee5-9321-47a3e76e71ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2248 :tutorial/title "Pyramids"} :step/id #uuid "0e005ac2-7765-5ed0-83c6-dc038809de33" :step/math-academy-id 9198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147294 :question/id #uuid "dc558592-e65f-5369-890d-fffc633f2a3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-2757"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddd0beef-ff0c-5ef3-88c4-ce49fbb8d5cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414713}] :knowledge-point/title "Naming Pyramids"} :step/id #uuid "4c62acce-9aa5-51e7-aab3-ddef11ded75b" :step/math-academy-id 9199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148719 :tutorial/content "Not all three-dimensional shapes are polyhedrons. Let's see some examples.\n\n- A **sphere** is the set of points with the same distance to a fixed point called the **center.** A sphere is a $3$-dimensional analog of a circle. A **hemisphere** is half of a sphere.\n\n![](images/84/84e7b63b5ec8761d4a3ac8a00b15a08f0dab7d4f95a536721df0c82e2999873f.png)\n\n- A **cylinder** is a solid shape with two parallel circular bases. There are two types of cylinders.A cylinder is **right** if the axis that passes through the centers of the circular bases is perpendicular to the bases. Otherwise, the cylinder is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/f8/f8ddb161d2d8628dd1294cc0d0042437300787217b518be73f010e79be90dcf7.png)\n\n- A **cone** is a solid shape with one circular base and one vertex called the **apex**. There are two types of cones.A cone is **right** if the axis that passes through the center of the circular base and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. Otherwise, the cone is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/2f/2f9e9ca18a36336ed99695bb51b167ffbd83f002139728f8dfe15227bf9f7745.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "daf27141-b818-5e08-b09c-b940a0ab763a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2250 :tutorial/title "Other Three-Dimensional Shapes"} :step/id #uuid "9570e433-348e-5b8c-8ac5-b5e75693bf4e" :step/math-academy-id 9201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148323}}] :activity/title "Identifying Three-Dimensional Shapes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147667}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414718} :activity/id #uuid "4fbea89c-8f67-5118-a1de-ee1d485d0bea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048313 :topic/id #uuid "93cbb547-bfa6-5649-8003-160f883bcb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 2467} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8744a0-0044-51b5-ba74-cb20ab4ca8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Heptagonal pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414682} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd46a53-d530-5265-b868-97591886f8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414683} {:answer/id #uuid "259cae62-6586-502d-bdf1-ccc26c4cad6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414684} {:answer/id #uuid "2687c571-e8a9-5532-9d9f-a9a82d818067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Heptagonal prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414685} {:answer/id #uuid "5737e950-c7ba-532c-b742-a233972b8975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Hexagonal prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414682} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4290576-9979-5457-9533-5ecbf8f69d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6fd9de3-145f-5992-abae-ba4ddaa8590f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93195" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5055d5290bfefa599d5fa357b76a3ccff05f9b0330c6610ce77deba949e34f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **heptagonal pyramid** since it is a pyramid whose base is a heptagon.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a triangular pyramid since not all its faces are triangular.\n\n- The solid is not a heptagonal prism since it has only one heptagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism because it has no hexagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a cube since it has triangular faces."} :step/id #uuid "0b209725-9e0d-563b-a187-f60cb609d0c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a030b9-4c33-563f-932d-cdf0103434a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6a/6a0e4fa080407695749f4e204f08cc062828f358573d56b2bc7e8a25fb61c2c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414680} {:answer/id #uuid "40e3dace-c8f0-5451-8326-07ac6adb342d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/18/180c9fc2f5c00706683921067bd3d4ced0e9f514df89ac1de66d1c959efd81cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414689} {:answer/id #uuid "488d6f82-d92d-5984-aa98-caa3998bb08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9b/9b094303688730cbd49075ad75faa3705e00999d40f253addfda88365991e401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414691} {:answer/id #uuid "b17cb05e-a4c9-58a6-946a-d88e0d16f2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/50/50679b5a5b333daae4346bf729ac2791a1024baed9ae580918d37925c11fe7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414697} {:answer/id #uuid "be92cffe-3554-5972-9564-de1dcf9aba22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9feb81a7255725b4d1b370012db8b7f06981daea8c96f16aa5fc6a59ae0a8898.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414691} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e137d3c-1309-5a42-8f77-b44643406daf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60a685f7-2a5b-5185-bd48-1abde4b2de57" :question/math-academy-id "q-69873" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/9b/9b094303688730cbd49075ad75faa3705e00999d40f253addfda88365991e401.png)\n\n    \n\nNotice that its left face is not a polygon (it's a circle)."} :step/id #uuid "4d46733c-4460-50dc-859b-7dd20a320ad8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb7f412-2103-58ff-a13b-06c1c4330e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/92/92151e8fc81b3c8b689b62ed0e5cf810b54f953d5a5675261b44d25d8419402a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414688} {:answer/id #uuid "44ffa52e-4d0a-566e-8284-0b15eba04159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d7/d79beefbd1605daf59fdb152c541784bd886019cc759d52dd6a1e92ffa3a0ff3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414690} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbb49d-5c4b-58fb-ace1-178d1cd464f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414695} {:answer/id #uuid "c68b03d2-d134-50f4-b325-4d5ddd31ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/03/037f72666076a57b9d20d9ec39a6c2e6efab31156a81d00ed419c6799c7056b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414700} {:answer/id #uuid "e351cfcb-2aac-5397-b2e7-de491bc69f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0b/0bd12e1073334c21ebd437e103c55d166b4d433d0eec46787bb1906014c0c41d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414702} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03ee3f1-4549-55c9-919e-e76bc4e6846f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c22d74f-c382-5fcc-9b04-2bd8cc4168b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117697" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/0b/0bd12e1073334c21ebd437e103c55d166b4d433d0eec46787bb1906014c0c41d.png)\n\n              \n\nNotice that some of its faces are not polygons (they have curved sides)."} :step/id #uuid "7bebf769-82ed-5ee5-a479-5d4cd518d762"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a28560e-80a3-57af-9dfa-e817b929bb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414687} {:answer/id #uuid "7cda9107-2b9b-5255-8de4-8a7f274925d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Right cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414696} {:answer/id #uuid "c366b12c-e909-516f-a328-bcc2b653608e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Oblique cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414699} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfdca69-53cd-540a-b90a-39a52dccc997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Oblique Cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414701} {:answer/id #uuid "ed96ecff-7047-526b-aea2-13644f032520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Right cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414704} :answer-field/id #uuid "8963e5d2-5f7d-506c-a501-21c462d94bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2449ad0f-383b-597c-b298-3099a99567bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-107276" :question/problem "![](images/26/264fb9324cad3bbfba25aa5a72c9c4a44e6c149b82f69d9e101fdff9e57a05d2.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **right cone**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has one circular base and one vertex. So, it's a cone.\n\n- The axis that passes through the center of the circle and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. So, it's right."} :step/id #uuid "87f1f045-6509-52aa-9286-2d5c94a46705" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1900d6b5-ab4e-5cb6-875c-d1325629dba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414681} {:answer/id #uuid "332e4dbe-20b8-54c1-9358-14a060035cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Pentagonal prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414686} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc569c-846f-5023-b24c-d641fdf00ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414693} {:answer/id #uuid "598ea44a-33fd-558b-a72c-9415a8e71931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Pentagonal pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414694} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a1f23e-e7bc-5e52-a9a0-4fe70a04cb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Rectangular prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414694} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5df61c8-1685-5b05-bfab-2db504a8dcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0def774f-f257-5952-add7-f3a6aeb741ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-61656" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **pentagonal pyramid** since it is a pyramid whose base is a pentagon.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a triangular pyramid since not all its faces are triangular.\n\n- The solid is not a pentagonal prism since it has only one pentagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles. Due to the same reasons, the solid is not a cube."} :step/id #uuid "d58ea3ef-a954-5458-b665-9faf042fb48e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414715}}] :activity/title "Identifying Three-Dimensional Shapes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414679}]}
